// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xxiii"] = {"qs": [["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "Annual disclosures by SSE-registered NPOs typically cover:", ["Governance, finances, programmes and impact", "Only the names of the NPO's donors and their addresses", "The share price of its corporate donors", "Its marketing plans for the next five years only"], 0, "Annual disclosures give stakeholders information on the governance structure, financial statements, programme activities and social impact, supporting transparency and accountability to donors."], ["SIA Reporting", "Reporting progress against baseline values in an impact report helps readers to:", ["Know the NPO's bank balance at the start of the year", "See how much has changed since the programme began", "Compare the NPO's rent with other NPOs", "Identify the auditor's personal qualifications"], 1, "A baseline sets the starting point for each indicator. Comparing endline with baseline, and ideally with a comparison group, shows the scale of change."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "Timely and regular disclosures by social enterprises benefit the SSE ecosystem mainly by:", ["Guaranteeing that every project will succeed", "Building trust and informing funding decisions", "Allowing NPOs to pay dividends to donors", "Removing the need for social auditors"], 1, "Regular disclosures reduce information gaps between enterprises and funders, encouraging more and better-targeted funding over time. They improve accountability but do not guarantee success."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "A for-profit social enterprise wishes to list equity through the SSE route. It must comply with:", ["Only the rules of the Ministry of Social Justice", "No rules, as social enterprises are exempt", "SEBI's ICDR issue norms plus SSE conditions", "Only the requirements of the Companies Act on CSR"], 2, "For-profit social enterprises follow the regular ICDR requirements for the chosen platform, plus additional SSE disclosures such as social intent and impact reporting."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "Theory of change is typically developed by:", ["Listing all activities and hoping they create impact", "Working backwards from the long-term goal", "Copying the budget of a similar organisation", "Starting from the funds available and ignoring goals"], 1, "Theory of change begins with the desired long-term change and maps the preconditions needed to reach it, making causal links and assumptions explicit."], ["Key Regulations", "Under the FCRA as amended in 2020, foreign contributions must first be received in:", ["Any savings account of the NPO in any bank of its choice", "The personal account of the NPO's chief executive", "A designated FCRA account at SBI's main New Delhi branch", "An account opened with a foreign bank outside India"], 2, "The 2020 amendment requires every FCRA-registered organisation to receive foreign contributions first in a designated account at the State Bank of India, New Delhi Main Branch."], ["Taxation", "For a company, CSR expenditure under the Companies Act is, for income-tax purposes, generally:", ["Fully deductible as a business expense in all cases", "Eligible for a deduction of twice the amount spent", "Not allowed as a business expense", "Exempt from GST and income tax in the hands of all recipients"], 2, "Income-tax law disallows CSR spending as a business expense, though some CSR contributions may qualify for separate donation deductions if conditions are met."], ["Key Regulations", "SEBI's provisions on issue of securities and fund raising by Social Enterprises are mainly found in:", ["The FCRA Rules issued by the Home Ministry", "SEBI ICDR Regulations", "The Income-tax Rules", "The Banking Ombudsman Scheme"], 1, "SEBI added a chapter on Social Stock Exchanges to the ICDR Regulations, covering registration, fund raising and related disclosures, with ongoing obligations under LODR."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "Which is a key difference between an SIA and a statutory financial audit?", ["SIA verifies the NPO's tax payments and GST returns", "SIA examines social outcomes, not financial statements", "SIA is conducted only by the income-tax department", "SIA checks share prices of listed social enterprises"], 1, "A financial audit gives an opinion on financial statements. SIA assesses whether claimed social results are real, measured credibly and attributable to the programme."], ["Taxation", "An adviser should avoid quoting income-tax section numbers to NPOs from memory mainly because:", ["The new Income-tax Act has changed numbering and rules", "NPOs are not covered by any tax law in India", "Section numbers are confidential", "SEBI prohibits mention of tax law"], 0, "India's new Income-tax Act has reorganised provisions, and Finance Acts regularly amend charity rules. Advice should be based on current law and professional verification."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "The SSE in India operates as:", ["A separate segment of a recognised stock exchange", "An independent exchange regulated by the RBI rather than SEBI", "A department of the Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "A private platform owned by a group of NGOs"], 0, "SEBI allowed recognised stock exchanges such as NSE and BSE to set up SSE segments. They are regulated by SEBI like other exchange segments."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "Compared with direct donations, raising funds through the SSE is expected to give donors:", ["A guaranteed financial return on their contribution", "Exemption from all KYC requirements", "Greater transparency through mandatory disclosures", "Control over the NPO's board decisions"], 2, "SSE-listed entities must make regular financial and impact disclosures, including independent social impact assessment. This gives donors more information than typical direct giving."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "The 'Most Significant Change' technique is primarily:", ["A statistical test of the difference between two group means", "A financial ratio comparing costs and benefits", "A qualitative method collecting stories of change", "A sampling formula for household surveys"], 2, "Most Significant Change gathers stakeholders' stories and systematically selects the most significant ones, capturing unexpected and qualitative outcomes that indicators may miss."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "To carry out social impact assessments for SSE-registered entities, an individual social auditor must generally:", ["Hold the NISM certification and be registered with a recognised SRO", "Be an employee of the NPO being assessed for at least three years", "Hold only a degree in social work without any registration", "Be approved by the NPO's largest donor"], 0, "SEBI's framework requires social auditors to hold the NISM Series XXIII certification and be registered with a self-regulatory organisation under a specified professional institute."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "Long-term, broad changes in a community, such as a reduction in district child mortality, are classified as:", ["Inputs", "Impact", "Activities", "Outputs"], 1, "Impact refers to long-term, wider changes that programmes contribute to, often alongside other factors. Attributing impact to one programme requires careful evaluation."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A microfinance programme reports high repayment rates, but interviews show many borrowers took new loans from moneylenders to repay. The assessor should conclude that:", ["High repayment rates always prove that borrowers are better off", "Moneylender loans are unrelated to the programme", "The programme should stop collecting repayment data", "Repayment alone may hide negative outcomes like over-indebtedness"], 3, "Output metrics like repayment can mask harm. Balanced assessment looks at household debt and wellbeing outcomes and reports unintended negative effects."], ["Taxation", "A for-profit social enterprise listed through the SSE framework is taxed:", ["At zero rate because it has social objectives", "Like any other company on its profits", "Only on dividends paid to shareholders", "Only if it raises money from foreign investors"], 1, "Social intent does not change a company's tax status. For-profit social enterprises pay corporate tax on profits under normal rules."], ["SIA Reporting", "Which presentation is most appropriate for reporting a sensitive outcome like reduced domestic violence?", ["Names and photos of survivors as proof", "Detailed addresses of households surveyed", "Unverified anecdotes from programme staff only", "Aggregated, anonymised data with context"], 3, "Sensitive outcomes need strong protection of respondents. Aggregated, anonymised data with qualitative context conveys the change achieved without risking harm or stigma to individuals."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "An NPO wants to raise funds through the SSE without offering any financial return. The suitable instrument is:", ["A listed non-convertible debenture paying 9% interest", "An IPO of equity shares on the main board", "A commercial paper issue placed with banks", "A ZCZP issue"], 3, "Most NPOs cannot issue shares or pay returns. ZCZP lets them raise donation-like funds through the exchange with SSE disclosure and impact reporting."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "Which professional skill is most important for a social auditor when interviewing beneficiaries in a rural setting?", ["Using leading questions to confirm what the NPO has claimed", "Interviewing only those beneficiaries chosen by the NPO staff", "Using culturally sensitive, unbiased questioning", "Conducting interviews in the presence of programme staff to save time"], 2, "Unbiased, context-appropriate questioning gives reliable data. Leading questions, NPO-selected respondents or staff presence can bias answers and weaken the assessment."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "The Impact Management Project's five dimensions of impact are:", ["What, Who, How Much, Contribution and Risk", "Input, Output, Outcome, Impact and Budget", "Plan, Do, Check, Act and Report", "Relevance, Efficiency, Tax, Audit and Compliance"], 0, "The IMP five dimensions describe what outcome occurs, who experiences it, how much change happens, the enterprise's contribution, and the risk that impact differs from expectations."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "A social auditor knowingly certifies an impact report containing false information. The auditor may face:", ["No consequences, since impact reports are voluntary", "Only a requirement to rewrite the report", "Disciplinary action by the SRO and action by SEBI", "Promotion to a senior position at the SRO"], 2, "Social auditors are accountable to their SRO and, under the SSE framework, to SEBI. Knowingly false certification is professional misconduct and may also breach securities law."], ["Key Regulations", "After the 2020 FCRA amendment, an NPO receiving foreign contribution may:", ["Freely transfer it to any registered NPO in India", "Use all of it for administrative expenses without limit", "Deposit it into any bank chosen by the donor", "Not transfer it to another organisation"], 3, "The 2020 amendment prohibits transfer of foreign contribution to any other person or organisation and lowered the cap on administrative expenses."], ["SIA Reporting", "A good social impact report should state its limitations because:", ["Limitations make the NPO's results look more impressive", "SEBI requires reports to hide all methodological problems", "Limitations replace the need to describe the methodology", "Readers need to judge how far findings can be relied on"], 3, "Stating limits — such as small samples or no comparison group — helps readers interpret findings correctly. It is a mark of credibility, not weakness."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A livelihood NGO claims that it 'lifted 10,000 families out of poverty' based only on a count of families enrolled. The weakness is that:", ["10,000 is too small a number to report", "Poverty cannot be measured under any method", "Enrolment is an output, not evidence of reduced poverty", "The NGO should have reported a larger figure"], 2, "Enrolment shows reach, not change. Claims about poverty reduction need outcome data such as income or consumption, compared against a baseline and ideally a counterfactual."], ["SIA Reporting", "An impact report shows only success stories and omits a project that missed its targets. This violates the principle of:", ["Confidentiality of beneficiary data", "Completeness and balance", "Timeliness of reporting", "Use of local language"], 1, "Reports must present a fair picture, including shortfalls and negative or unintended effects. Selective reporting misleads donors and investors and damages the credibility of the whole report."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "Investors in a Zero Coupon Zero Principal (ZCZP) instrument should understand that:", ["They receive a fixed coupon every year and principal at maturity", "Their principal is guaranteed by the stock exchange", "They can trade the instrument daily on the equity segment", "They receive neither interest nor principal back"], 3, "ZCZP instruments work like donations through the exchange: no interest and no repayment of principal. The return to investors is the social impact the NPO delivers."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "A for-profit social enterprise listed on an exchange through the SSE route must comply with:", ["Normal listing obligations plus impact reporting", "Only impact reporting and no financial disclosures", "No obligations once its shares are listed", "Only the disclosure rules for mutual funds"], 0, "For-profit social enterprises are listed companies, so they must follow SEBI's listing regulations in full, along with the additional SSE-specific social impact disclosures."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "Outcome-based funding, such as a development impact bond, links payments to:", ["The number of staff the NGO employs during the year", "The size of the NGO's office and its infrastructure", "Achievement of agreed social results", "The amount of money spent, regardless of what it achieved"], 2, "In outcome-based funding, investors provide upfront capital and outcome funders pay only when independently verified results are achieved. It shifts the focus from inputs to outcomes."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A skilling NGO reports that 80% of trainees found jobs. The assessor finds that local job demand doubled because a large factory opened nearby. The key issue is:", ["Drop-off — the jobs will certainly disappear next year", "Displacement — trainees took jobs from people abroad", "Inputs — the NGO spent too little on training", "Attribution — part of the result may be due to the factory"], 3, "External factors like a new factory can explain outcomes. The assessor should estimate the programme's contribution, for example using a comparison group from areas without the factory."], ["SIA Reporting", "A social auditor cannot obtain enough evidence to support some of the NPO's claims. In the assessment report, the auditor should:", ["Issue a clean opinion to avoid upsetting donors", "Qualify the opinion and explain the limitation", "Delete the claims from the NPO's own report without telling anyone", "Refuse to issue any report and keep the fee"], 1, "When evidence is insufficient, the auditor should modify or qualify the opinion and explain the reason, so readers understand what has and has not been assured."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "Why do SSE norms require NPOs to show a minimum operating history before registration?", ["To ensure the NPO has paid dividends to its members", "To prove that the NPO is owned by a listed company", "To show a track record of programmes and governance", "To check whether the NPO has listed equity shares"], 2, "A track record gives donors evidence of the NPO's experience and credibility. NPOs do not pay dividends, and ownership by a listed company is not a criterion."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "An NPO that has raised funds through the SSE must disclose periodically:", ["A statement of how the funds have been used", "Its daily bank balance on the exchange website", "The personal income tax returns of its trustees", "A list of every beneficiary with their phone numbers"], 0, "NPOs must report utilisation of funds raised against the objects stated in the fund-raising document, with any deviations explained. Personal data of beneficiaries must not be published."], ["Key Regulations", "An NPO collecting beneficiaries' personal data must comply with:", ["The SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations", "The Negotiable Instruments Act", "The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023", "The Payment and Settlement Systems Act"], 2, "The DPDP Act governs processing of digital personal data, requiring lawful purpose, consent or legitimate use, and safeguards. It applies to NPOs collecting beneficiary data."], ["SIA Case Studies", "An NPO's school programme compares test scores of its students with district averages, but its schools are in wealthier blocks. The main problem is:", ["Too many students in the sample", "Use of test scores as an indicator", "Insufficient project budget", "Selection bias in the comparison"], 3, "Students in wealthier blocks may score higher regardless of the programme. A credible comparison needs groups with similar backgrounds, such as matched schools."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "The NPO pressures the social auditor to remove a finding of poor outcomes from the report. The auditor should:", ["Retain the finding if supported by evidence", "Remove it, since the NPO is paying for the assessment", "Replace it with a positive finding from another NPO", "Delete all data so that no one can verify it"], 0, "Objectivity requires reporting supported findings even if unwelcome. Bowing to client pressure undermines the purpose of SIA and may lead to disciplinary action."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "Why do disclosure norms require reporting both deviations in fund utilisation and their reasons?", ["So donors can judge whether changes were justified", "To allow the NPO to avoid future impact reports", "To give the exchange a reason to raise fees", "To replace the need for annual financial audits"], 0, "Explaining deviations — such as budget shifts due to a natural disaster — lets stakeholders assess management decisions. It does not replace audits or impact reports."], ["SIA Case Studies", "An assessor finds that attendance registers in a health programme were filled in by staff days later in the same handwriting. The best response is to:", ["Treat the data as unreliable and seek other evidence", "Use the registers as they are, since they are official records", "Ask staff to rewrite them neatly before the assessment", "Ignore attendance altogether in the report without mention"], 0, "Signs of fabricated or back-filled records undermine reliability. The assessor should triangulate with other sources, such as beneficiary interviews, and report the data quality issue."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "If a ZCZP issue fails to collect its minimum subscription, the NPO should:", ["Refund the money to the subscribers", "Keep the money and use it for any other project it chooses", "Transfer the money to the stock exchange's general account", "Convert the money into a loan repayable after ten years"], 0, "Like other public issues, a ZCZP issue that does not meet its minimum subscription cannot proceed, and funds must be refunded to subscribers."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "Failure to submit required disclosures on time may lead to:", ["Automatic increase in the NPO's grant funding", "Exemption from future disclosure requirements", "Transfer of the NPO's assets to the exchange", "Exchange action such as fines or suspension"], 3, "Exchanges monitor compliance and, under SEBI's framework, may impose fines, suspend trading or registration, or delist entities for persistent non-compliance with disclosure norms."], ["Key Regulations", "A Section 8 company is restricted from:", ["Opening a bank account in its own name", "Paying dividends to its members", "Employing staff and paying salaries", "Receiving donations from Indian donors"], 1, "Section 8 companies must apply profits to promote their objects and cannot pay dividends. They can open bank accounts, employ staff and receive donations."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A sanitation programme built 2,000 toilets, but a survey shows only 900 are regularly used. In the report, the assessor should present:", ["2,000 toilets as the final impact achieved", "Toilets built as output and regular use as outcome", "Only the 900 figure and hide the output", "Neither figure, since data differ"], 1, "Toilets built is an output; regular use is the behavioural outcome that leads to health impact. Reporting both shows the gap and areas for improvement."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "A listed company's corporate foundation, which runs health camps in villages, wants to raise ZCZP funds on the SSE. What is the position?", ["It may register, provided its parent company guarantees the ZCZP issue", "Corporate foundations are excluded from the SSE framework", "It may register only if it first converts into a for-profit social enterprise", "It may register, since every health activity automatically qualifies"], 1, "SEBI's framework specifically excludes corporate foundations, along with political or religious organisations and professional or trade associations, even if their activities are social. Independent NPOs serving target groups may qualify."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "In a girls' education programme, 'number of bicycles distributed to girls' is best classified as:", ["An input to the programme", "A long-term impact on society", "An external assumption", "An output"], 3, "Outputs are the direct products of activities, such as bicycles distributed. The resulting change, like higher attendance, is an outcome, while long-term change is impact."], ["SIA Reporting", "Which element best supports the verifiability of a reported outcome?", ["A photograph of the NPO's office building", "A quote from the NPO's chairperson praising the programme", "A clear audit trail to source data and method", "A list of awards the NPO has received"], 2, "Verifiable reporting allows a third party to trace numbers back to data and methods. Photos, quotes and awards do not substantiate outcomes on their own."], ["Key Regulations", "Schedule VII of the Companies Act, 2013 lists:", ["Taxes payable by NPOs on business income", "Activities eligible as CSR spending", "Members of the board of the SSE", "Rules for issuing equity shares"], 1, "Schedule VII sets out activities that qualify as CSR, such as education, health, environment and rural development. Companies must spend CSR funds on these activities."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "Funds raised by an NPO through the SSE must be utilised:", ["For any purpose decided later by the NPO's founder", "To repay the founders' personal loans", "To buy listed shares for capital gains", "For the projects stated in the fund-raising document"], 3, "Utilisation must follow the objects disclosed to donors, and the NPO must report utilisation periodically. Diverting funds breaches SSE norms and may attract action."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "To qualify as a Social Enterprise, an entity must show that a substantial share of its activities targets underserved populations. The test is commonly measured using:", ["The number of board members who are retired government officers", "Revenue, expenditure or beneficiaries from target groups", "The size of its head office and number of branches", "Its share price performance in the past three years"], 1, "SEBI's framework requires a minimum proportion (currently 67%) of activities, measured through revenue, expenditure or customer/beneficiary base, to come from or serve the target population."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "An NPO raising funds through a ZCZP issue must file a document disclosing its projects, use of funds and risks. This document is the:", ["Red herring prospectus for an equity IPO on the main board", "Scheme information document of a mutual fund", "GST return for the previous quarter", "Fund-raising document filed with the SSE"], 3, "NPOs file a draft and final fund-raising document for ZCZP issues, describing the project, target segment, fund utilisation and risks. This is lighter than an equity prospectus."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "OECD-DAC's 'sustainability' criterion asks whether:", ["Benefits are likely to continue after support ends", "The NPO uses recycled paper in its offices", "The programme cost less than its budget", "The programme reached the planned number of people"], 0, "Sustainability looks at whether net benefits persist after the intervention or funding ends. Cost and reach are covered under efficiency and effectiveness."], ["SIA Reporting", "The Annual Impact Report (AIR) of an SSE-registered entity must be:", ["Prepared by the stock exchange on the entity's behalf", "Kept confidential and shared only with the founders", "Submitted only once every five years", "Assessed by a social auditor and disclosed"], 3, "The AIR describes the entity's impact against its strategy and targets and must be assessed by an eligible social auditor and disclosed to the SSE for stakeholders."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "Corporate Social Responsibility spending under the Companies Act, 2013 is based on:", ["Total revenue of the company in the current financial year only", "The market capitalisation of the company on the last trading day", "The number of employees the company has in India", "Average net profits of the preceding three years"], 3, "Companies meeting the CSR thresholds must spend at least 2% of their average net profit for the three immediately preceding financial years on eligible CSR activities."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "Why has credible impact measurement become important in the social sector?", ["Because SEBI requires all NGOs in India to list on stock exchanges", "Because impact reports replace the need for financial audits", "Because measurement guarantees that every programme will succeed", "Funders want evidence that money creates real change"], 3, "Donors, CSR teams and impact investors increasingly allocate funds based on demonstrated results. Impact assessment complements, but does not replace, financial audits."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "A social auditor's main responsibility in an SIA is to:", ["Raise funds for the NPO from new donors and investors", "Prepare the NPO's financial statements and tax returns", "Give an independent opinion on reported impact", "Decide which beneficiaries the NPO should serve"], 2, "The social auditor independently examines the evidence behind claimed outputs and outcomes and gives an assurance opinion. Fund-raising, accounting and programme design are the NPO's job."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "Funds, staff and training materials used by a programme are its:", ["Outcomes", "Impacts", "Inputs", "Outputs"], 2, "Inputs are the resources put into a programme. Activities use inputs to produce outputs, which lead to outcomes and, ultimately, impact."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "Which indicator is the best example of an outcome indicator for a maternal health programme?", ["Number of awareness sessions held in the villages", "Share of births attended by skilled health workers", "Amount of money spent on printing posters", "Number of field staff hired during the year"], 1, "Outcomes measure changes in behaviour or conditions among beneficiaries. Sessions held, money spent and staff hired describe activities or inputs."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "A job-training programme places trainees in local jobs that would otherwise have gone to other unemployed residents of the same town. Which SROI adjustment captures this?", ["Displacement", "Deadweight, since some trainees would have found jobs anyway", "Drop-off, since job retention falls over the years", "Attribution to other organisations working in the town"], 0, "Displacement occurs when a programme's benefit shifts outcomes from elsewhere, such as trainees taking jobs others would have got. Deadweight, drop-off and attribution are different adjustments."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A nutrition programme reduces child stunting from 40% to 30%, while a comparison area fell from 40% to 36%. The impact attributable to the programme is about:", ["10 percentage points", "4 percentage points", "6 percentage points", "30 percentage points"], 2, "Difference-in-differences: programme change (−10) minus comparison change (−4) = −6 percentage points. The other 4 points would likely have happened anyway."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "Social Impact Funds under the SEBI AIF Regulations are a sub-category of:", ["Category III AIFs that use leverage to trade listed securities", "Mutual fund schemes open to all retail investors", "Portfolio management services for high-net-worth clients", "Category I AIFs"], 3, "Social Impact Funds (formerly Social Venture Funds) are Category I AIFs investing in social enterprises. They can also receive grants and may route funds to SSE-registered entities."], ["Disclosure Norms, Reporting & Penalties", "Primary responsibility for the accuracy of an SSE-registered entity's disclosures lies with the:", ["Stock exchange where it is registered", "Entity and its management", "Social auditor alone", "Donors who contributed funds"], 1, "The entity prepares and owns its disclosures. Social auditors give an opinion on impact reports, and exchanges monitor compliance, but neither replaces management's responsibility."], ["Taxation", "The main tax benefit of registration of a charitable trust as an approved charitable institution is that:", ["Income applied for charitable purposes is exempt, subject to conditions", "The trust can distribute its surplus to trustees tax-free", "All business income of the trust becomes fully exempt without conditions", "The trust no longer needs to maintain any accounts"], 0, "Registered charitable institutions can claim exemption on income applied to their objects, if they meet conditions such as application of income, accounts and audit. Surplus cannot be distributed."], ["Taxation", "Anonymous donations received by a charitable trust are generally:", ["Always fully exempt, regardless of amount", "Required to be returned to the donor", "Treated as the trustee's personal income", "Subject to special tax rules beyond limits"], 3, "Tax law restricts anonymous donations to discourage misuse, taxing them above specified limits, with some exceptions for wholly religious bodies. Proper donor records are essential."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "An NPO's SSE registration may lapse or be withdrawn if it:", ["Fails to meet ongoing disclosure obligations", "Achieves more beneficiaries than it originally planned", "Receives donations from many small donors", "Opens a new office in another state"], 0, "Registered entities must keep complying with disclosure and eligibility conditions. Persistent non-compliance can lead to suspension or withdrawal of registration."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "Propensity score matching is used when:", ["The programme has no beneficiaries and no data are available", "The assessor wants to avoid using any comparison group", "Random assignment is not possible and a matched comparison group is needed", "All beneficiaries are identical in every characteristic"], 2, "PSM matches participants with similar non-participants based on observed characteristics, creating a comparison group that approximates what would have happened without the programme."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "Which of the following is a for-profit model in the social sector?", ["A public charitable trust registered under a state trusts law", "A social enterprise selling solar lamps at affordable prices", "A society running a free school under the Societies Registration Act", "A Section 8 company that cannot distribute profits to members"], 1, "A social enterprise can earn and distribute profits while pursuing social goals. Trusts, societies and Section 8 companies are not-for-profit forms that cannot distribute surplus to members."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "While collecting beneficiary data, a social auditor should:", ["Publish beneficiaries' names and addresses to prove authenticity", "Collect as much personal information as possible for future use", "Share the data with marketing companies to fund the audit", "Obtain informed consent and protect personal data"], 3, "Ethical assessment requires informed consent, data minimisation and confidentiality, consistent with India's data protection law. Publishing personal details can harm vulnerable people."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "A logic model's 'assumptions' are best described as:", ["The final results the programme guarantees to deliver to donors", "Conditions that must hold for each step to lead to the next", "Budget lines that list how money will be spent", "Names of staff responsible for each activity"], 1, "Assumptions are beliefs about conditions, like 'trained farmers will have access to water'. If they fail, the expected chain from outputs to outcomes may break."], ["Key Regulations", "Public charitable trusts in many states, such as Maharashtra, are governed by:", ["The SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "The Banking Regulation Act", "The Insurance Act", "State public trust laws"], 3, "Public trusts are regulated by state laws, such as the Maharashtra Public Trusts Act. The Indian Trusts Act, 1882 mainly governs private trusts."], ["Taxation", "A charitable trust that carries on a business incidental to its objects must take care that:", ["It distributes the business profits to its trustees", "It stops filing income tax returns", "Business receipts stay within prescribed limits", "It converts into a partnership firm"], 2, "Charitable institutions may undertake activities in the nature of trade only within limits and conditions. Exceeding them can jeopardise exemption."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "In a randomised control trial (RCT), the key feature that supports causal claims is that:", ["Only the most motivated villages are selected for the programme", "Participants are randomly assigned to treatment or control", "Results are measured only for those who complete the programme", "The NPO chooses which beneficiaries receive services based on need"], 1, "Random assignment makes treatment and control groups similar on average, so differences in outcomes can be attributed to the programme rather than selection effects."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "The UN Sustainable Development Goals are primarily intended to:", ["Replace national laws on charities and social enterprises", "Set binding tax rates for NGOs in member countries", "Provide a shared global framework for development", "Rate individual NGOs for donors on a scale of 1 to 10"], 2, "The SDGs set 17 goals with targets to be achieved by 2030. Governments, companies and NGOs use them to align and communicate their work, but they are not laws or ratings."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "Which statement about 'blended finance' is correct?", ["It combines concessional and commercial capital", "It refers to NGOs mixing cash and in-kind donations in one account", "It is a type of loan given only by banks to individuals", "It means investing only in listed equity shares"], 0, "Blended finance uses philanthropic or public money, often taking first loss or giving guarantees, to attract private investors into projects that would otherwise seem too risky."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "A development finance institution (DFI) differs from a typical philanthropic foundation mainly because a DFI:", ["Usually expects its capital to be repaid or earn a return", "Never invests in private sector companies of any kind", "Gives only small grants to individual households", "Is not owned or backed by any government"], 0, "DFIs, often government-backed, provide loans, equity and guarantees to projects with development goals, and generally expect repayment or returns. Foundations typically give grants."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "Under the SSE framework, the social impact assessment of an entity is meant to give comfort mainly to:", ["Donors, investors and other stakeholders", "The NPO's competitors seeking to copy its programmes", "The tax department in computing income tax", "The stock exchange's trading members alone"], 0, "SIA gives stakeholders independent assurance that funds raised through SSE are creating the social impact claimed, improving trust and accountability."], ["SIA Reporting", "In an impact report, materiality means focusing on:", ["Only outcomes that are easiest to measure", "Only figures that look largest", "Outcomes that matter most to stakeholders' decisions", "Outcomes the NPO's founder personally prefers"], 2, "Material information is what could influence stakeholders' decisions about funding or support. Reports should prioritise significant outcomes, not just convenient or flattering ones."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "A SMART indicator is one that is:", ["Simple, monetary, annual, regulatory and tax-related", "Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound", "Social, moral, accountable, reliable and transparent", "Standard, mandatory, audited, reported and tracked"], 1, "SMART indicators are precisely defined, can be measured, are realistic, relate to the programme's goals and have a time frame, making progress tracking meaningful."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "A typical eligibility condition for an NPO seeking SSE registration is that it:", ["Has been incorporated as a listed public limited company", "Has at least 10,000 employees on its rolls", "Has made a profit in each of the last five years", "Holds valid tax-exemption registrations"], 3, "NPOs must be legally registered, hold valid registrations for income-tax exemption and donor deductions, and meet operating history and financial conditions. Profit or size is not required."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "An NPO invests ₹40 lakh, and the present value of social benefits, after adjustments, is ₹1.4 crore. Its SROI ratio is:", ["1.4 : 1", "0.29 : 1", "3.5 : 1", "4.0 : 1"], 2, "SROI = present value of benefits ÷ investment = 140 ÷ 40 = 3.5. Every rupee invested generates an estimated ₹3.50 of social value, after adjustments like deadweight and attribution."], ["SSE: Introduction, Funding Structures & Instruments", "A for-profit social enterprise that wants to raise money through the SSE framework may issue:", ["Zero Coupon Zero Principal instruments meant only for NPOs", "Insurance policies with guaranteed maturity benefits", "Units of a mutual fund scheme managed by itself", "Equity shares or debt securities"], 3, "For-profit social enterprises raise capital through regular securities such as equity or debt under SEBI rules, subject to SSE conditions. ZCZP instruments are available only to NPOs."], ["Registration and Listing on SSE", "Which statement correctly describes the relationship between SSE registration and fund-raising for an NPO?", ["Registration is a precondition but does not oblige it to raise funds", "Registration automatically lists ZCZP instruments for the NPO", "Registration is optional for NPOs wishing to issue ZCZP", "Registration permits the NPO to issue equity shares"], 0, "An NPO must register on the SSE before raising funds there, but it may remain registered without raising money, subject to annual disclosure requirements."], ["Social Impact Assessment & Assessors", "A social auditor previously worked as a paid consultant designing the same programme she is now asked to assess. She should:", ["Accept, since her knowledge of the programme is an advantage", "Accept, but not tell the NPO's donors about her past role", "Disclose the conflict and decline if independence is impaired", "Ask the NPO to pay a higher fee to compensate for the risk"], 2, "Assessing one's own work creates a self-review threat. Independence rules require disclosure and, where the threat cannot be managed, declining the engagement."], ["Logic Model in Social Programs", "A programme trains 1,000 farmers, but crop yields do not improve because farmers cannot afford the recommended seeds. This reveals:", ["A failed assumption between output and outcome", "That the programme's inputs were too large", "That training is never useful for farmers", "That impact always follows outputs automatically"], 0, "The training (output) was delivered, but the expected outcome needed an unmet condition — access to affordable seeds. Logic models help spot such gaps early."], ["SIA Frameworks, Techniques & Standards", "A difference-in-differences design compares:", ["Only post-programme outcomes of beneficiaries with national averages", "Changes over time in programme and comparison groups", "Budgets of two NPOs working in different states", "Responses from staff before and after training"], 1, "DiD subtracts the comparison group's change from the programme group's change, removing common trends. It assumes both groups would have followed parallel trends without the programme."], ["Key Regulations", "A company's unspent CSR amount relating to an ongoing project must be:", ["Distributed to shareholders as a special dividend", "Kept in the company's general reserves indefinitely", "Paid to the company's directors as a bonus", "Moved to a special unspent CSR account and spent within the set period"], 3, "CSR rules require unspent amounts for ongoing projects to be transferred to an Unspent CSR Account and used within a specified period; other unspent sums go to specified funds."], ["SIA Case Studies", "A women's self-help group programme shows income gains in year 1 that decline by half by year 3. In SROI, this pattern is captured through:", ["Drop-off", "Deadweight", "Displacement", "Leverage"], 0, "Drop-off accounts for the decline of outcomes over time. Deadweight is what would have happened anyway, and displacement is benefit shifted from others."], ["Taxation", "A donor contributing to an NPO with valid donor-deduction approval can generally:", ["Receive a refund of the full donation from the government", "Claim a deduction even for cash donations of any size", "Claim a deduction for eligible donations", "Avoid filing an income tax return for that year"], 2, "Eligible donations to approved institutions qualify for a deduction, subject to limits and conditions. Large cash donations above a threshold are not eligible, so donors should use banking channels."], ["Taxation", "Why must an NPO apply most of its income towards its objects in the year of receipt or accumulate it as permitted?", ["To retain its tax exemption", "To qualify for a higher interest rate on deposits", "To be listed on the main board of the exchange", "To avoid preparing a balance sheet"], 0, "Exemption depends on applying a prescribed share of income to charitable purposes or accumulating it for specified purposes with intimation. Failure can lead to tax on unapplied income."], ["Social Sector Ecosystem", "An impact investor is best distinguished from a traditional investor by its intention to:", ["Avoid any financial return and give money away as grants", "Generate measurable social impact alongside returns", "Invest only in government bonds with guaranteed returns", "Earn the highest return possible regardless of social effects"], 1, "Impact investors intentionally seek measurable positive social or environmental outcomes together with financial returns, which may range from below-market to market rates."]]};
